## Write the Audiovisual Brief

The brief is the last step, but it is the one that decides whether the sessions work. Give your supplier these items:

-   **The rooms.** Which rooms, how many people in each and the layout.
-   **The sessions.** How many run at the same time and when each one starts and ends.
-   **The speakers.** How many speak, whether they stay at a lectern or walk, and whether the audience asks questions from the floor.
-   **The languages.** Whether any session needs interpretation.
-   **The venue.** Its address, the loading access and who we contact there.

With that, we match your event to our own inventory. The [MICE Pack](/packages/mice/) at 490 euros ex VAT, for up to 120 guests, includes professional active speakers, a 60 inch LED display, one gooseneck plus one wireless handheld microphone and a technician on site for up to 6 hours. A lectern is an extra at 50 euros and modular stage platforms are 35 euros per square meter. The [Basic MICE Pack](/packages/basic-mice/) at 295 euros ex VAT is for up to 40 guests. For a larger room or several rooms at once, tell us the program and we check what we can supply from our own inventory and what we would source.

The technician operates the sound and the microphones. The speaker runs their own slides.

## What to Check With the Venue

A room with some equipment of its own is common, so ask before you hire anything. Ask which sound, screens and microphones are included, who operates them and what has to come from outside. Ask about loading access, the time you can enter the room and any rules of the house, so the delivery fits the building. For a multilingual congress, ask whether the room has interpretation booths, since some venues do.

### Technical Rehearsal Before a Corporate Event

A technical rehearsal is a run through the room with the real equipment before the doors open, and it is worth asking the venue for it whenever the room lets you in early. Ask for access once the sound and the screen are installed, so the technician and the speakers can test together instead of meeting for the first time in front of the delegates.

Go through what the sessions depend on:

-   **Microphones.** Test each wireless handheld, the gooseneck and any lapel set where the speaker will actually stand, and walk the room for dead spots or feedback.
-   **Screen or projector.** Connect the speaker's own laptop, tell us the connection it needs and check that the slides are readable from the back row.
-   **Music and audio.** Play the walk on music or a video's sound through the system and set the level.
-   **Speaker changes.** Practice the handover, who advances the slides and who calls the next speaker, as laid out in your [run of show](/blog/mice-events-in-malaga/).

We cannot promise rehearsal time in the room, so ask for it in the brief and we confirm what we can do.

## Who Does What in Malaga

-   **The Malaga Convention Bureau.** Its 2024 report lists technical advice for organizing congresses, availability of venues and accommodation and local logistics among the requests it handled.
-   **The venue.** The room, its own equipment and its rules.
-   **An agency or a DMC.** Transport, the social program and the logistics of a group, if you use one. We work with whichever you choose.
-   **Top Group Express.** Group hotel reservations.
-   **Malaga Event Gear.** Sound, a display or a projector, microphones and a technician. Simultaneous interpretation and voting systems are not in our own inventory, and we coordinate them with a subcontracted partner.

